# Shell scripts must keep LF, whatever the checkout platform does by default.
#
# This is not a style preference. Every .sh here is mounted into a Linux container and executed
# there — platform/docker/demo/provision.sh is bind-mounted into curlimages/curl and run as the
# demo provisioner. Git's core.autocrlf=true, which is the default on Windows, rewrites these to
# CRLF on checkout, and the container then runs a script whose every line ends in a carriage
# return: `sh` carries the \r into arguments and filenames, and a shebang becomes a request for an
# interpreter named "/bin/sh\r" that does not exist.
#
# The repository blobs were already LF; it was the working copy that differed, so this file changes
# what lands on disk rather than what is stored. Run `git add --renormalize .` after adding it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Mounted into a Linux container and run there too (scripts/shoot.sh copies it into the Playwright
# image). Node tolerates CRLF where sh does not, so this is consistency rather than a live bug.
*.mjs text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: read inside containers, not by Windows tooling.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
Caddyfile* text eol=lf
mvnw text eol=lf

# Windows-only by definition, and PowerShell is content either way — pinned so it does not flip
# back and forth depending on who cloned it.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
